{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Parathyroid cancer is a rare disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of a parathyroid gland.","source_span":"Parathyroid cancer is a rare disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of a parathyroid gland."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"The parathyroid glands are four pea-sized organs found in the neck near the thyroid gland.","source_span":"The parathyroid glands are four pea-sized organs found in the neck near the thyroid gland."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"The parathyroid glands make parathyroid hormone (PTH), which helps the body use and store calcium to keep blood calcium at normal levels.","source_span":"The parathyroid glands make parathyroid hormone (PTH or parathormone). PTH helps the body use and store calcium to keep the calcium in the blood at normal levels."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Hyperparathyroidism is most often caused by a benign tumor called an adenoma, and only very rarely by parathyroid cancer.","source_span":"Hyperparathyroidism can occur when a benign tumor (noncancer), called an adenoma, forms on one of the parathyroid glands, and causes it to grow and become overactive. Sometimes hyperparathyroidism can be caused by parathyroid cancer, but this is very rare."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Extra PTH causes hypercalcemia by moving calcium from bones into the blood and increasing intestinal absorption of calcium.","source_span":"The extra PTH causes: - The calcium stored in the bones to move into the blood. - The intestines to absorb more calcium from the food we eat... This condition is called hypercalcemia"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"The hypercalcemia caused by hyperparathyroidism is more serious and life-threatening than parathyroid cancer itself.","source_span":"The hypercalcemia caused by hyperparathyroidism is more serious and life-threatening than parathyroid cancer itself and treating hypercalcemia is as important as treating the cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Risk factors for parathyroid cancer include the inherited disorders familial isolated hyperparathyroidism (FIHP) and multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome.","source_span":"Risk factors for parathyroid cancer include the following rare disorders that are inherited (passed down from parent to child): - Familial isolated hyperparathyroidism (FIHP). - Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Treatment with radiation therapy may increase the risk of developing a parathyroid adenoma.","source_span":"Treatment with radiation therapy may increase the risk of developing a parathyroid adenoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of parathyroid cancer include weakness, feeling tired, and a lump in the neck.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of parathyroid cancer include weakness, feeling tired, and a lump in the neck."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Other signs and symptoms of parathyroid cancer include pain in the abdomen/side/back, bone pain, a broken bone, change in voice such as hoarseness, and trouble swallowing.","source_span":"Other signs and symptoms of parathyroid cancer include the following: - Pain in the abdomen, side, or back that doesn't go away. - Pain in the bones. - A broken bone. - A lump in the neck. - Change in voice such as hoarseness. - Trouble swallowing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"A sestamibi scan injects a small amount of the radioactive substance technetium 99 into a vein to find an overactive parathyroid gland.","source_span":"A very small amount of a radioactive substance called technetium 99 is injected into a vein and travels through the bloodstream to the parathyroid gland."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","statement":"Diagnostic procedures for parathyroid cancer include physical exam and history, blood chemistry studies, parathyroid hormone test, sestamibi scan, CT scan, SPECT scan, ultrasound exam, angiogram, and venous sampling.","source_span":"Blood chemistry studies... Parathyroid hormone test... Sestamibi scan... CT scan... SPECT scan... Ultrasound exam... Angiogram... Venous sampling"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What are the parathyroid glands and what hormone do they produce?","answer":"The parathyroid glands are four pea-sized organs in the neck near the thyroid gland. They produce parathyroid hormone (PTH), which helps the body use and store calcium to keep blood calcium at normal levels."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What two inherited disorders are risk factors for parathyroid cancer?","answer":"Familial isolated hyperparathyroidism (FIHP) and multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome are inherited risk factors for parathyroid cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"What condition results from too much PTH, and why is it considered more dangerous than the cancer itself?","answer":"Too much PTH causes hypercalcemia (too much calcium in the blood), which moves calcium from bones into the blood and increases intestinal calcium absorption. This hypercalcemia is more serious and life-threatening than parathyroid cancer itself."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","fact_refs":[10],"question":"Which imaging test uses the radioactive substance technetium 99 to locate an overactive parathyroid gland?","answer":"A sestamibi scan, which injects a small amount of technetium 99 into a vein so it collects in and highlights the overactive parathyroid gland."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","fact_refs":[8,9],"question":"What are the common signs and symptoms of parathyroid cancer?","answer":"Common signs include weakness, feeling tired, and a lump in the neck, along with abdominal/side/back pain, bone pain, broken bones, hoarseness, and trouble swallowing."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000033_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Can radiation therapy affect the risk of developing parathyroid conditions?","answer":"Yes, treatment with radiation therapy may increase the risk of developing a parathyroid adenoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Penile cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the penis.","source_span":"Penile cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the penis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"The penis contains two types of erectile tissue: the corpora cavernosa (two columns forming most of the penis) and the corpus spongiosum (single column).","source_span":"Corpora cavernosa: The two columns of erectile tissue that form most of the penis. - Corpus spongiosum: The single column of erectile tissue that forms a small portion of the penis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"The corpus spongiosum surrounds the urethra, the tube through which urine and sperm pass from the body.","source_span":"The corpus spongiosum surrounds the urethra (the tube through which urine and sperm pass from the body)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Human papillomavirus (HPV) infection may increase the risk of developing penile cancer.","source_span":"Human papillomavirus infection may increase the risk of developing penile cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Circumcision may help prevent HPV infection, and men who were not circumcised at birth may have a higher risk of developing penile cancer.","source_span":"Circumcision may help prevent infection with the human papillomavirus (HPV)... Men who were not circumcised at birth may have a higher risk of developing penile cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Other risk factors for penile cancer include being age 60 or older, having phimosis, poor personal hygiene, having many sexual partners, and using tobacco products.","source_span":"Being age 60 or older. - Having phimosis (a condition in which the foreskin of the penis cannot be pulled back over the glans). - Having poor personal hygiene. - Having many sexual partners. - Using tobacco products."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Signs of penile cancer include sores, discharge, and bleeding, as well as redness, irritation on the penis, or a lump on the penis.","source_span":"Signs of penile cancer include sores, discharge, and bleeding... Redness, irritation, or a sore on the penis. - A lump on the penis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Biopsy methods used to diagnose penile cancer include fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy, incisional biopsy, and excisional biopsy.","source_span":"Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy: The removal of tissue or fluid using a thin needle. - Incisional biopsy... - Excisional biopsy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for penile cancer depend on the stage of the cancer, the location and size of the tumor, and whether the cancer is newly diagnosed or has recurred.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - The stage of the cancer. - The location and size of the tumor. - Whether the cancer has just been diagnosed or has recurred (come back)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"Penile cancer staging uses five stages: Stage 0 (Carcinoma in Situ), Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV.","source_span":"The following stages are used for penile cancer: - Stage 0 (Carcinoma in Situ) - Stage I - Stage II - Stage III - Stage IV"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","statement":"A sentinel lymph node biopsy removes the first lymph node likely to receive cancer spread from a tumor, located using a radioactive substance and/or blue dye injected near the tumor.","source_span":"The sentinel lymph node is the first lymph node to receive lymphatic drainage from a tumor. It is the first lymph node the cancer is likely to spread to from the tumor. A radioactive substance and/or blue dye is injected near the tumor."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Besides HPV infection, what other risk factors are associated with penile cancer?","answer":"Other risk factors include being age 60 or older, having phimosis, poor personal hygiene, having many sexual partners, and using tobacco products."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the signs of penile cancer?","answer":"Signs of penile cancer include sores, discharge, and bleeding, as well as redness, irritation, or a lump on the penis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are the five stages used to classify penile cancer?","answer":"Penile cancer is staged as Stage 0 (Carcinoma in Situ), Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"How does circumcision relate to penile cancer risk?","answer":"Circumcision may help prevent HPV infection, and men who were not circumcised at birth may have a higher risk of developing penile cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What factors affect the prognosis and treatment options for penile cancer?","answer":"Prognosis and treatment depend on the stage of the cancer, the location and size of the tumor, and whether the cancer is newly diagnosed or has recurred."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What is a sentinel lymph node biopsy and how is the node identified during penile cancer staging?","answer":"It is the removal of the sentinel lymph node, the first lymph node likely to receive cancer spread from the tumor; it is located by injecting a radioactive substance and/or blue dye near the tumor and tracking where it first flows."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"A pituitary tumor is a growth of abnormal cells in the tissues of the pituitary gland.","source_span":"A pituitary tumor is a growth of abnormal cells in the tissues of the pituitary gland."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"The pituitary gland is a pea-sized organ in the center of the brain, just above the back of the nose, sometimes called the \"master endocrine gland.\"","source_span":"Pituitary tumors form in the pituitary gland, a pea-sized organ in the center of the brain, just above the back of the nose. The pituitary gland is sometimes called the \"master endocrine gland\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"Pituitary tumors are divided into three groups: benign pituitary adenomas, invasive pituitary adenomas, and pituitary carcinomas.","source_span":"Pituitary tumors are divided into three groups: - Benign pituitary adenomas... - Invasive pituitary adenomas... - Pituitary carcinomas"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"Very few pituitary tumors are malignant (pituitary carcinomas).","source_span":"Very few pituitary tumors are malignant."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"Most pituitary tumors are functioning tumors, which make more than the normal amount of one or more hormones.","source_span":"Functioning pituitary tumors make more than the normal amount of one or more hormones. Most pituitary tumors are functioning tumors."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"Hormones made by the pituitary gland include prolactin, adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), growth hormone, thyroid-stimulating hormone, and luteinizing hormone (LH)/follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH).","source_span":"Hormones made by the pituitary gland include: - Prolactin... - Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)... - Growth hormone... - Thyroid-stimulating hormone... - Luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"Risk factors for pituitary tumors include the hereditary diseases multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome, Carney complex, and isolated familial acromegaly.","source_span":"Risk factors for pituitary tumors include having the following hereditary diseases: - Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome. - Carney complex. - Isolated familial acromegaly."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"In adults, too much growth hormone from a pituitary tumor may cause acromegaly, the growth of bones in the face, hands, and feet; in children it may cause excessive overall body growth.","source_span":"Too much growth hormone may cause... In adults, acromegaly (growth of the bones in the face, hands, and feet). In children, the whole body may grow much taller and larger than normal."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"Signs of a pituitary tumor include problems with vision and certain physical changes.","source_span":"Signs of a pituitary tumor include problems with vision and certain physical changes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","statement":"Too much ACTH from a pituitary tumor may cause weight gain in the face, neck, and trunk with thin arms and legs, a fat lump on the back of the neck, thin skin with stretch marks, and easy bruising.","source_span":"Too much ACTH may cause: - Headache. - Some loss of vision. - Weight gain in the face, neck, and trunk of the body, and thin arms and legs. - A lump of fat on the back of the neck. - Thin skin that may have purple or pink stretch marks on the chest or abdomen. - Easy bruising."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the three categories used to classify pituitary tumors?","answer":"Pituitary tumors are divided into benign pituitary adenomas, invasive pituitary adenomas, and pituitary carcinomas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What hereditary diseases increase the risk of developing a pituitary tumor?","answer":"Risk factors include multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome, Carney complex, and isolated familial acromegaly."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What condition can too much growth hormone from a pituitary tumor cause in adults versus children?","answer":"In adults it can cause acromegaly, the growth of bones in the face, hands, and feet; in children, the whole body may grow much taller and larger than normal."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Where is the pituitary gland located and why is it called the \"master endocrine gland\"?","answer":"The pituitary gland is a pea-sized organ in the center of the brain, just above the back of the nose; it is called the \"master endocrine gland\" because it makes hormones that affect many parts of the body and controls hormones made by other glands."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What hormones does the pituitary gland produce?","answer":"The pituitary gland produces prolactin, adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), growth hormone, thyroid-stimulating hormone, and luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000035_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What symptoms can result from a pituitary tumor producing too much ACTH?","answer":"Too much ACTH may cause weight gain in the face, neck, and trunk with thin arms and legs, a fat lump on the back of the neck, thin skin with stretch marks, and easy bruising."}
